<section class="how">
    <h2>How to Calculate Your Weighted or Unweighted GPA</h2>
    <p>This GPA calculator simplifies tracking your academic standing for both high school and college students. Start by selecting your format: use Unweighted for a standard 4.0 scale, or Weighted if you want to include extra points for AP, IB, or Honors classes.</p>
    <p>In the interactive table, optionally name your courses to stay organized. Then, move left to right, choosing your actual or expected letter grade from the dropdown and entering the credit hours. For college courses, this is usually 3 or 4 credits. If you selected standard high school classes, leave it at 1 credit. The algorithm works silently in the background, updating your cumulative grade point average instantly as you alter any field.</p>
</section>

<section class="formula">
    <h2>Understanding the GPA Formula</h2>
    <p>Educational institutions translate letter grades into rigid numeric multipliers. On a standard unweighted scale, an A provides 4.0 points, a B provides 3.0 points, a C provides 2.0, and so on. Your total quality points for a class are calculated by multiplying that point value by the course credit hours.</p>
    <p>To discover your definitive grade point average, the formula sums together all of your earned quality points and divides that total by the total number of credit hours attempted. This calculator strictly mirrors the exact numeric standards used by major administrative registrar offices, ensuring your results match your official transcript.</p>
</section>

<section class="examples">
    <h2>Weighted vs. Unweighted GPA</h2>
    <p>High school administrators employ two distinct grading scales to evaluate student performance, particularly to reward those taking advanced coursework.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Unweighted GPA Calculator:</strong> This represents the universally standardized calculation where the maximum boundary is exactly 4.0. It measures your performance without factoring in the difficulty of your classes. An 'A' in an introductory class holds the same weight as an 'A' in an advanced placement class.</li>
        <li><strong>Weighted GPA Calculator:</strong> This alternative formatting rewards ambitious students pursuing advanced placement (AP), international baccalaureate (IB), or honors programming. The ceiling typically breaks past 4.0, often allowing up to 5.0 points for an 'A' in a collegiate-level high school course.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

